WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.413
A counter-deception response begins with preservation and containment of harm.

2
00:00:04.413 --> 00:00:12.358
Preserve relevant evidence before systems rotate logs, messages are deleted, tickets are modified, or links are shared further.

3
00:00:12.358 --> 00:00:16.330
At the same time, avoid amplifying the questionable claim.

4
00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:21.184
Do not forward sensitive artifacts broadly just because they seem important.

5
00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:29.570
Use approved channels, need-to-know sharing, and clear handling labels when privacy, legal, human resources, or investigative concerns may apply.

6
00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.100
Verification should use trusted channels and defined ownership.

7
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.955
If the claim involves identity, use identity and access management records.

8
00:00:37.955 --> 00:00:42.810
If it involves a vendor, use procurement or vendor management contacts.

9
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:47.665
If it involves a security incident, use the incident command structure.

10
00:00:47.665 --> 00:00:52.519
If it involves potential fraud, use the fraud or risk process.

11
00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:56.491
The analyst does not need to own every decision.

12
00:00:56.491 --> 00:01:03.994
The analyst needs to route the question to the right owner with the right evidence and uncertainty.

13
00:01:03.994 --> 00:01:07.084
Communication should separate facts, assumptions, and judgments.

14
00:01:07.084 --> 00:01:10.173
Facts are observed records or preserved artifacts.

15
00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:13.704
Assumptions are working beliefs that may be tested.

16
00:01:13.704 --> 00:01:17.676
Judgments are analytic conclusions based on the current evidence.

17
00:01:17.676 --> 00:01:28.268
A useful update might say what was claimed, which artifacts were preserved, which sources were checked, what was corroborated, what remains unknown, what confidence

18
00:01:28.268 --> 00:01:32.240
level is appropriate, and what decision is needed next.

19
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.447
Governance makes the practice repeatable.

20
00:01:34.447 --> 00:01:43.273
Playbooks should define escalation paths, evidence handling, privacy boundaries, chain of custody expectations, confidence language, approval workflows, and after-action review.

21
00:01:43.273 --> 00:01:48.570
Metrics should reward timely verification and good documentation, not just fast closure.

22
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:59.162
After each meaningful review, improve the playbook: which signal mattered, which source helped, which step caused delay, and which control would make the next

23
00:01:59.162 --> 00:02:00.044
decision cleaner.
